Q7. What is the output? Explain.
int x = 2;
switch (x) {
    case 1: System.out.println("One");
    case 2: System.out.println("Two");
    case 3: System.out.println("Three");
    default: System.out.println("Default");
}

1. What is the output?
int x = 1;
switch (x) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
        System.out.println("One or Two");
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Three");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Other");
}

2. Rewrite this classic switch as an enhanced switch expression:
int month = 4;
int days;
switch (month) {
    case 2: days = 28; break;
    case 4: case 6: case 9: case 11: days = 30; break;
    default: days = 31;
}
System.out.println(days);

items.a:
int month = 4;
int days = switch (month) {
    case 2          -> 28;
    case 4, 6, 9, 11 -> 30;
    default         -> 31;
};
System.out.println(days); // 30

The enhanced version uses comma-separated case labels (case 4, 6, 9, 11) eliminating stacked empty cases, no break required, and the switch directly produces the value that is assigned to days. Much cleaner.

